## Solution

We identify the integrand with the Laurent coefficient formula
$$a_n = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_C \frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\,dz.$$

With $z_0=0$ and the denominator $z^4 = z^{3+1}$, we have $n=3$, so
$$I = a_3\bigl[f(z)\bigr],\qquad f(z)=2(1-\cos z)e^z.$$

**$f$ is analytic at $0$** (since $1-\cos z$ has a zero of order $2$ there, cancelling the pole), so $a_3 = \dfrac{f^{(3)}(0)}{3!}$.

### Computing $f^{(3)}(0)$

Let $g(z)=1-\cos z$ and $h(z)=e^z$, so $f=2gh$. The derivatives at $0$:

| $k$ | $g^{(k)}(0)$ | $h^{(k)}(0)$ |
|---|---|---|
| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |
| $1$ | $\sin 0 = 0$ | $1$ |
| $2$ | $\cos 0 = 1$ | $1$ |
| $3$ | $-\sin 0 = 0$ | $1$ |

By the Leibniz rule,
$$(gh)''' = g'''h + 3g''h' + 3g'h'' + gh'''.$$

Evaluating at $0$:
$$(gh)'''(0) = (0)(1) + 3(1)(1) + 3(0)(1) + (0)(1) = 3.$$

Hence
$$f^{(3)}(0) = 2\cdot (gh)'''(0) = 2\cdot 3 = 6.$$

### The integral

$$I = a_3 = \frac{f^{(3)}(0)}{3!} = \frac{6}{6} = 1.$$

### Verification via series

$2(1-\cos z) = z^2 - \dfrac{z^4}{12} + \cdots$ and $e^z = 1 + z + \dfrac{z^2}{2}+\dfrac{z^3}{6}+\cdots$. The coefficient of $z^3$ in the product comes only from $z^2\cdot z = z^3$, giving coefficient $1$. All higher terms of $2(1-\cos z)$ start at $z^4$ and cannot contribute to $z^3$. This confirms $a_3=1$.

$$\boxed{I = 1}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
